{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"2861af9a73966356710244eddb70a4f66935eca474db9f6aff03cfaf8fe6acc5","digest":{"component_or_area":"opcert_service app lifespan worker","key_entities":["src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py","tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py","asyncio.wait_for","asyncio.timeout","asyncio.to_thread","test_matching_guard_is_accepted","test_worker_cancellation_survives_job_completion_race","_worker","CPython issue 86296"],"problem_keywords":["python 3.11","asyncio","cancellation","xdist","worker crash","hang","testclient"],"propositions":["Python 3.11 asyncio.wait_for consumes external cancellation when child future completes in same turn, leaving worker with pending cancellation count and lifespan awaiting forever.","TestClient teardown hangs after test_matching_guard_is_accepted on Python 3.11 because opcert worker does not promptly terminate when job completion collides with shutdown cancellation.","Replacing asyncio.wait_for with asyncio.timeout semantics resolves the cancellation consumption issue and allows opcert worker to exit cleanly on Python 3.11.","Regression oracle test_worker_cancellation_survives_job_completion_race must remain RED before fix and GREEN after, with exact defect-seeded mutation returning it to RED."]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787329890073191001,"uuid":"b317d1cf-2880-4745-9372-8a23131dd999"}